Presidential elections were held in [[Austria]] on 25 April 2010,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.salzburg.com/online/7mal24/aktuell/Fischer-kandidiert-fuer-zweite-Amtszeit.html?article=eGMmOI8VdICFv2WnLJLamXN6M4RBlSXzlcfsP0r&img=&text=&mode=&|title=Fischer kündigt Kandidatur per Video an|date=23 November 2009|access-date=25 April 2010|publisher=[[Salzburger Nachrichten]]|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110720235524/http://www.salzburg.com/online/7mal24/aktuell/Fischer-kandidiert-fuer-zweite-Amtszeit.html?article=eGMmOI8VdICFv2WnLJLamXN6M4RBlSXzlcfsP0r&img=&text=&mode=&|archive-date=20 July 2011|url-status=dead}}</ref> the twelfth election of an Austrian head of state since 1951. The candidates were President [[Heinz Fischer]] (independent, formerly [[Social Democratic Party of Austria|SPÖ]]), [[Barbara Rosenkranz]] ([[Freedom Party of Austria|FPÖ]]) and Rudolf Gehring ([[Christian Party of Austria|CPÖ]]). Heinz Fischer won with just under 80% of the valid votes. Voter turnout was a historic low of 54%.

==Candidates==
===[[Social Democratic Party of Austria]]===
Incumbent President [[Heinz Fischer]] ([[Independent politician|independent]], former member of the [[Social Democratic Party of Austria|Social Democratic Party]]) announced on 23 November 2009 that he will seek a second term.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://derstandard.at/1256745420089/Fischer-kandidiert-erneut|title=Fischer kandidiert erneut|date=23 November 2009|access-date=25 April 2010|work=[[Der Standard]]|archive-date=28 February 2010|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100228060129/http://derstandard.at/1256745420089/Fischer-kandidiert-erneut|url-status=live}}</ref> According to a 27 June 2009, [[Gallup poll]] for the newspaper [[Österreich (newspaper)|Österreich]], Fischer had a job approval rating of 80%.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.oe24.at/oesterreich/politik/Fischer_bei_Praesidenten-Frage_in_Front_0486150.ece|title=Fischer bei Präsidenten-Frage in Front|date=27 June 2009|access-date=25 April 2010|publisher=oe24.at|archive-date=29 June 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090629222032/http://www.oe24.at/oesterreich/politik/Fischer_bei_Praesidenten-Frage_in_Front_0486150.ece|url-status=live}}</ref>

===[[Austrian People's Party]]===
There were rumours that the candidate of the [[Austrian People's Party]] would be either Governor of [[Lower Austria]] [[Erwin Pröll]] or president of the [[Austrian Federal Economic Chamber]] [[Christoph Leitl]]. On 13 October 2009 Pröll announced that he would not run for the presidency and that he would remain Governor of Lower Austria.<ref>{{cite web|author=Quinn, Kathryn|url=http://www.wienerzeitung.at/DesktopDefault.aspx?TabID=4082&Alias=wzo&cob=444218|title=Pröll will not run for presidency|date=13 October 2009|access-date=25 April 2010|publisher=[[Wiener Zeitung]]}}</ref> Leitl and former governor of Styria [[Waltraud Klasnic]] also declined a presidential run.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://derstandard.at/1254312026461/Klasnic-will-nicht-VP-Kandidatin-werden|title=Klasnic will nicht VP-Kandidatin werden|date=21 October 2009|access-date=25 April 2010|work=[[Der Standard]]|archive-date=3 March 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160303185659/http://derstandard.at/1254312026461/Klasnic-will-nicht-VP-Kandidatin-werden|url-status=live}}</ref> On 25 February 2010, the People's Party announced that it would not nominate a candidate, and would not officially support any candidate.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://derstandard.at/1266541375748/Bundespraesident-OeVP-ueberlaesst-Fischer-die-Hofburg-kampflos|title=ÖVP überlässt Fischer die Hofburg kampflos|date=24 February 2010|access-date=25 April 2010|work=[[Der Standard]]| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20100501092225/http://derstandard.at/1266541375748/Bundespraesident-OeVP-ueberlaesst-Fischer-die-Hofburg-kampflos| archive-date= 1 May 2010 | url-status= live}}</ref>

===[[Freedom Party of Austria]]===
The [[Freedom Party of Austria|Freedom Party]] announced in June 2009 that it would definitely field a candidate "to prevent Fischer['s reelection]". Possible candidates mentioned included [[Martin Graf]], [[Norbert Steger]], [[Siegfried Dillersberger]], [[Norbert Gugerbauer]], [[Dieter Böhmdorfer]] and [[Wilhelm Brauneder]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://kurier.at/nachrichten/1927505.php |title=Hier stimmt etwas nicht |access-date=25 April 2010 |publisher=[[Kurier (Austrian newspaper)|Kurier]] |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091006030710/http://www.kurier.at/nachrichten/1927505.php |archive-date=6 October 2009 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://derstandard.at/1245820154369/Bundespraesidentenwahl-FPOe-stellt-Kandidaten-auf|title=FPÖ stellt Kandidaten auf|date=26 June 2009|access-date=25 April 2010|work=[[Der Standard]]|archive-date=30 June 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090630074839/http://derstandard.at/1245820154369/Bundespraesidentenwahl-FPOe-stellt-Kandidaten-auf|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://derstandard.at/1244460960335/FPOe-Organisationen-uebergeben-Unterschriften-an-Graf|title=FPÖ-Organisationen übergeben Unterschriften an Graf|date=18 June 2009|access-date=25 April 2010|work=[[Der Standard]]|archive-date=21 June 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090621132529/http://derstandard.at/1244460960335/FPOe-Organisationen-uebergeben-Unterschriften-an-Graf|url-status=live}}</ref> After the Carinthian branch of the [[Alliance for the Future of Austria]] [[The Freedomites in Carinthia|broke away to re-unite]] with the Freedom Party, it was rumoured that [[Barbara Rosenkranz]] would be their candidate.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://news.orf.at/?href=http%3A%2F%2Fnews.orf.at%2Fticker%2F351960.html|title=Zeitung: Rosenkranz soll für FPÖ/FPK um Hofburg kämpfen|access-date=25 April 2010|publisher=news.ORF.at|archive-date=18 July 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110718111029/http://news.orf.at/?href=http:%2F%2Fnews.orf.at%2Fticker%2F351960.html|url-status=live}}</ref> In early January 2010, [[Heinz-Christian Strache]], leader of the Freedom Party, guaranteed that the party would nominate a candidate to assure that Fischer would not run unopposed.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://orf.at/?href=http%3A%2F%2Forf.at%2Fticker%2F353116.html|title=Hofburg: FPÖ garantiert Fischer-Gegenkandidaten|access-date=25 April 2010|publisher=news.ORF.at|archive-date=11 June 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110611192212/http://orf.at/?href=http:%2F%2Forf.at%2Fticker%2F353116.html|url-status=live}}</ref> On 28 February 2010, Strache announced in the ''[[Kronen Zeitung]]'' that the Freedom Party would nominate Barbara Rosenkranz as its presidential candidate.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.krone.at/krone/S32/object_id__187536/hxcms/index.html|title=Strache schickt Rosenkranz ins Rennen um Hofburg|date=27 February 2010|access-date=25 April 2010|publisher=[[Kronen Zeitung]]|archive-date=2 March 2010|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100302205023/http://www.krone.at/krone/S32/object_id__187536/hxcms/index.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

===[[Alliance for the Future of Austria]]===
[[Alliance for the Future of Austria]] leader [[Josef Bucher]] and General Secretary [[Stefan Petzner]] voiced their support for a presidential candidacy of [[Claudia Haider]], widow of former Alliance leader [[Jörg Haider]], by inviting [[The Greens – The Green Alternative|the Greens]] and the Freedom Party to form a non-partisan committee. [[Carinthia (state)|Carinthia]] Governor [[Gerhard Dörfler]] spoke out against such a candidacy, because he "wouldn't like to join forces with Heinz-Christian Strache's [Freedom Party]", but nonetheless said that Claudia Haider would be a "good candidate, who could finance her campaign on her own". On 17 August, Haider announced that she would not run for president.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.oe24.at/oesterreich/politik/BZOe-Streit_um_Claudia_Haider_0483218.ece|title=BZÖ-Streit um Claudia Haider|date=22 June 2009|access-date=25 April 2010|publisher=oe24.at|archive-date=27 July 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090727022954/http://www.oe24.at/oesterreich/politik/BZOe-Streit_um_Claudia_Haider_0483218.ece|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.oe24.at/oesterreich/politik/Haiders-Witwe-kandidiert-nicht-0514866.ece|title=Haiders Witwe kandidiert nicht|date=17 August 2009|access-date=25 April 2010|publisher=oe24.at|archive-date=22 September 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090922050308/http://www.oe24.at/oesterreich/politik/Haiders-Witwe-kandidiert-nicht-0514866.ece|url-status=live}}</ref> On 26 February 2010, Bucher said that he might run as a candidate for his party, because "he could appeal to [People's Party] voters".<ref>{{cite web|url=http://derstandard.at/1267131863958/BZOe-Wahlkampf-um-600000-Euro-moeglich|title=BZÖ: Wahlkampf um 600.000 Euro möglich|date=26 February 2010|access-date=25 April 2010|work=[[Der Standard]]|archive-date=1 March 2010|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100301224523/http://derstandard.at/1267131863958/BZOe-Wahlkampf-um-600000-Euro-moeglich|url-status=live}}</ref> On 1 March 2010, the Alliance announced that Bucher would not stand as a candidate, as the odds were clearly against him and they did not want to waste taxpayers' money.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://derstandard.at/1267132052800/Rueckzug-BZOe-schickt-niemanden-ins-Rennen|title=BZÖ schickt niemanden ins Rennen|date=1 March 2010|access-date=25 April 2010|work=[[Der Standard]]| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20100508001842/http://derstandard.at/1267132052800/Rueckzug-BZOe-schickt-niemanden-ins-Rennen| archive-date= 8 May 2010 | url-status= live}}</ref>

===[[The Greens – The Green Alternative]]===
It was expected that former [[The Greens – The Green Alternative|Green Party]] leader [[Alexander Van der Bellen]] might stand in the election, but he stated in January 2009 that if Fischer ran for reelection he would have his support.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://oe1.orf.at/inforadio/101213.html?filter=3 |title=Archived copy |access-date=18 January 2009 |archive-date=5 June 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110605042252/http://oe1.orf.at/inforadio/101213.html?filter=3 |url-status=dead }}</ref> In June 2009, however, Green party leader [[Eva Glawischnig]] stated that the Greens were considering fielding a candidate, and that Van der Bellen would be a very good candidate.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://oe1.orf.at/inforadio/108140.html?filter=3|title=Seite nicht gefunden|access-date=25 April 2010|publisher=[[Ö1]]|archive-date=5 June 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110605042832/http://oe1.orf.at/inforadio/108140.html?filter=3|url-status=live}}</ref> By November 2009, there was still no clear decision from the Greens, even though Van der Bellen had ruled out a run for the presidency on 23 November 2009.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://derstandard.at/1252037015584/Bundespraesident-Gruene-ueber-eigenen-Kandidaten-unschluessig|title=Grüne über eigenen Kandidaten unschlüssig|date=10 September 2009|access-date=25 April 2010|work=[[Der Standard]]|archive-date=24 November 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091124154811/http://derstandard.at/1252037015584/Bundespraesident-Gruene-ueber-eigenen-Kandidaten-unschluessig|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://derstandard.at/1256745427688/Gruene-Entscheidung-Van-der-Bellen-tritt-nicht-an|title=Van der Bellen tritt nicht an|date=23 November 2009|access-date=25 April 2010|work=[[Der Standard]]| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20100402092826/http://derstandard.at/1256745427688/Gruene-Entscheidung-Van-der-Bellen-tritt-nicht-an| archive-date= 2 April 2010 | url-status= live}}</ref> On 25 February 2010, the Greens announced they wanted to concentrate on state elections and would not nominate a candidate.<ref>{{cite web|author1=Prior, Thomas|author2=Pink, Oliver|name-list-style=amp|url=http://diepresse.com/home/politik/innenpolitik/542552/index.do?_vl_backlink=/home/index.do|title=Hofburg: Parteiinterne Kritik an ÖVP-Verzicht|date=25 February 2010|access-date=25 April 2010|publisher=[[Die Presse]]|archive-date=18 November 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211118090421/https://www.diepresse.com/542552/hofburg-parteiinterne-kritik-an-ovp-verzicht?_vl_backlink=%2Fhome%2Findex.do|url-status=live}}</ref> After Fischer appeared at an event where the Greens questioned him on a number of issues, with questions submitted by voters via the internet, the Greens on 12 April 2010 officially endorsed his candidacy and called for their voters to vote for him, a first in the party's history.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.gruene.at/topstories/artikel/lesen/60946/|title=Bundespräsidentschaft: Grüne empfehlen offiziell Wahl Fischers|date=12 April 2010|access-date=25 April 2010|publisher=[[The Greens – The Green Alternative]]|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100417224820/http://www.gruene.at/topstories/artikel/lesen/60946/|archive-date=17 April 2010|url-status=dead}}</ref>

===[[Christian Party of Austria]]===
The [[Christian Party of Austria|Christian Party]] nominated its chairman [[Rudolf Gehring]] as a presidential candidate.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://derstandard.at/1259281230069/Gehring-Christen-Partei-stellt-Kandidaten-auf|title=Christen-Partei stellt Kandidaten auf|date=4 December 2009|access-date=25 April 2010|work=[[Der Standard]]|archive-date=10 February 2010|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100210192736/http://derstandard.at/1259281230069/Gehring-Christen-Partei-stellt-Kandidaten-auf|url-status=live}}</ref> On 25 March 2010, they announced they had gathered the necessary number signatures to run for president.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.kleinezeitung.at/nachrichten/politik/2320734/drei-kandidaten-rittern-um-hoechstes-amt-staat.story |title = Drei Kandidaten rittern um h chstes Amt im Staat > Kleine Zeitung  |access-date=25 March 2010 |archive-date=27 March 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100327073739/http://www.kleinezeitung.at/nachrichten/politik/2320734/drei-kandidaten-rittern-um-hoechstes-amt-staat.story |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Other candidates===
A former judge from eastern [[Styria]], [[Martin Wabl]], who had twice previously tried to run for president, announced on 6 February 2009 that he would run again.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://derstandard.at/1233586845626|title=Martin Wabl will sich wieder bewerben|date=6 February 2009|access-date=25 April 2010|work=[[Der Standard]]}}</ref> Green municipal councillor [[Ulrich Habsburg-Lothringen]], as a descendant of [[House of Habsburg|the former ruling house]] of [[Austria-Hungary]] incapacitated by constitutional law to run for Austria's presidency, stated that he wanted to test the law at Austria's constitutional court if his candidacy is not permitted. [[Bernhard Gregor Honemann]] (nominated by the platform agenda2020.at) also planned to run.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://derstandard.at/1267132010108/Habsburg-Kandidatur-Zuversicht-fuer-Unterstuetzungserklaerungen|title=Zuversicht für Unterstützungserklärungen|date=1 March 2010|access-date=25 April 2010|work=[[Der Standard]]|archive-date=4 March 2010|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100304134109/http://derstandard.at/1267132010108/Habsburg-Kandidatur-Zuversicht-fuer-Unterstuetzungserklaerungen|url-status=live}}</ref> None of these candidates obtained the 6000 signatures necessary to run for president. The signatures submitted were:<ref>{{cite web|url=http://kurier.at/nachrichten/1989438.php |title="Sehr schönes Ergebnis" für Heinz Fischer |access-date=25 April 2010 |publisher=[[Kurier (Austrian newspaper)|Kurier]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100331135720/http://kurier.at/nachrichten/1989438.php |archive-date=31 March 2010 |url-status=dead }}</ref>
* Fischer: 45,000 signatures
* Rosenkranz: 10,500 signatures
* Gehring: 8,000 signatures

==Voter statistics==
According to the federal election commission, 6,355,800 Austrian citizens aged 16+ were eligible to vote in the presidential election. Compared with the 2004 presidential election, the number of eligible voters increased by 324,818, or 5.4%&nbsp;– primarily due to lowering the legal voting age to 16 between the two elections. 3,307,366 women and 3,048,434 men were eligible to vote.<ref>{{cite web|url= http://www.bmi.gv.at/cms/BMI_wahlen/bundespraes/bpw_2010/Wahlberechtigte_E.aspx|title= Bundespräsidentenwahl 2010 – endgültige Zahl der Wahlberechtigten|language= de|publisher= [[Federal Ministry of the Interior (Austria)|Federal Ministry of the Interior]]|access-date= 30 April 2010| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20100428221806/http://www.bmi.gv.at/cms/BMI_wahlen/bundespraes/bpw_2010/Wahlberechtigte_E.aspx| archive-date= 28 April 2010 | url-status= live}}</ref>

Total number of eligible voters by state:

* Burgenland: 231,257
* Carinthia: 447,679
* Lower Austria: 1,267,379
* Upper Austria: 1,092,760
* Salzburg: 391,142
* Styria: 973,009
* Tyrol: 530,265
* Vorarlberg: 265,026
* Vienna: 1,157,283

